# Copy this file to .env (already gitignored) and fill in the placeholders below.
# docker-compose.yml pulls these in automatically since .env sits next to it.
#
# Powers issue #59's forgot-password email — without these set, the app still runs fine, but
# "Forgot password?" responds with a clear "email isn't configured" error instead of sending mail.

# Brevo's free tier (https://www.brevo.com) via its transactional HTTP API, not SMTP (issue #95) —
# many hosts, including Railway below its Pro plan, block outbound SMTP ports outright, which plain
# HTTPS (what this API uses) never runs into. Up to ~300 emails/day free, and the only provider
# evaluated here that lets you verify a single sender EMAIL ADDRESS rather than a whole domain,
# which matters since this app has no domain of its own. This does mean the app is tied to Brevo
# specifically now, not "any SMTP relay" — a deliberate trade, see mailer.ts's header comment.

# Brevo dashboard -> SMTP & API -> API Keys tab -> generate a new key.
SNOWPRO_EMAIL_API_KEY=your-generated-brevo-api-key

# The "From" address on every email this app sends — must be a sender you've verified in Brevo's
# dashboard (Senders, Domains & Dedicated IPs -> Senders) before Brevo will actually deliver through it.
SNOWPRO_EMAIL_FROM=your-verified-sender@example.com

# Optional — the display name shown alongside SNOWPRO_EMAIL_FROM. Defaults to "SnowPro Core Prep".
# SNOWPRO_EMAIL_FROM_NAME=Your Name

# The admin-added-user login link always uses the live address you're actually browsing on when
# you trigger the email -- no static config needed for that one (see CLAUDE.md's "Emailed links
# always use the live request address" section for why).
#
# REQUIRED, though, for the self-registration welcome email and the forgot-password reset email
# specifically (issue #139) -- those two are triggered by an unauthenticated request, where an
# attacker could otherwise spoof the Host header on a request naming someone else's email and get
# a link mailed to that victim pointing at an attacker-chosen domain. Without this set, those two
# emails are skipped (logged, not silently swallowed) rather than built from an untrusted header.
SNOWPRO_HOST_NAME=your-custom-hostname

# Optional — Google OAuth login (issue #113), an additional sign-in option alongside email+
# password, not a replacement. Without these set, the app still runs fine — LoginGate.tsx's
# "Continue with Google" button just never appears (GET /api/oauth/google/available reports it
# unavailable) rather than showing a button that would 404 on click.
#
# Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials -> Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID
# -> Web application. Authorized redirect URIs: for local dev,
# http://localhost:8080/api/oauth/google/callback (Google allows plain http for localhost as a
# documented exception); for a public deployment, https://<your-domain>/api/oauth/google/callback.
# SNOWPRO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
# SNOWPRO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret

# Optional — accounts that should always be administrators (issue #159), comma-separated.
# Without it, admin goes to the first account created on a fresh database. That is fine on a LAN
# box, where the operator is the only person who can reach it — but on a PUBLIC deployment it means
# the first stranger to sign up after a fresh deploy becomes the administrator before you ever
# visit. Setting this removes that race instead of narrowing it, and unlike a "first account" rule
# it survives a database reset.
#
# Applied at boot (promoting an account that already exists) and at signup (so the named account is
# admin from the moment it is created, whichever sign-in method it uses). Grant-only: it never
# demotes anyone, so a typo or a momentarily-unset value can't strip your own admin rights and lock
# you out. Removing an admin stays a deliberate act via /admin or the admin CLI.
# SNOWPRO_ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com,co-maintainer@example.com

# Optional — "Explore the demo" guest mode (issue #160). Lets a visitor use the real app without
# registering, on a throwaway account reaped after SNOWPRO_GUEST_TTL_DAYS of inactivity.
#
# DEFAULT OFF, and intentionally so: it adds an endpoint that writes a database row with no
# authentication at all, which a self-hosted LAN box gains nothing from — the people on your
# network were invited by you and already have accounts. Turn it on for a public deployment, where
# a signup form standing between a link and the product is what actually costs you visitors.
#
# This is also the fast kill switch. Because releases only publish on a version tag, flipping this
# off and restarting is the quickest way to shut the endpoint down if it is ever abused.
# SNOWPRO_ENABLE_GUEST=1
#
# Inactivity window before a guest account is eligible for deletion. Clamped to a minimum of 1 —
# a value of 0 would make every live guest immediately reapable, which is the one setting mistake
# here with irreversible consequences.
# SNOWPRO_GUEST_TTL_DAYS=7
#
# Cap on simultaneously-live guest accounts. At the cap the endpoint refuses new demos (asking the
# visitor to create a free account) rather than evicting an existing one, which could drop someone
# midway through a 115-minute mock exam.
# SNOWPRO_GUEST_MAX_LIVE=500
#
# "log" (the default) reports what the reaper WOULD delete without deleting anything; "delete"
# enacts it. Run a new deployment in log mode for one release and read the logs before switching:
# this is the only thing in the app that removes user rows on its own.
# SNOWPRO_GUEST_REAP=log

# --- Legal pages: who operates this deployment (issue #182) ---------------------------------
#
# These publish /privacy/, /terms/, /about/, /accessibility/, /security/ and
# /.well-known/security.txt.
#
# LEAVE THEM UNSET AND NO LEGAL PAGES ARE GENERATED AT ALL. That is deliberate and it is the
# safe default, not an oversight: a privacy policy names a real, legally answerable operator. If
# these had defaults, every deployment of this image would publish a policy naming somebody else
# as the controller of ITS users' data — confidently wrong, and it points erasure requests at a
# stranger. A private or LAN instance has no public audience and needs none of this.
#
# Set all three together on a public deployment. Any one missing and the pages stay off; the
# container logs which state it is in at boot.
#
# SNOWPRO_LEGAL_OPERATOR=Your Name
# SNOWPRO_LEGAL_CONTACT=you@example.com
#
# Absolute origin, scheme and host only — no path, no trailing slash. Used for canonical URLs and
# for security.txt's Canonical field, so it must be the address people actually reach this at.
# SNOWPRO_SITE_ORIGIN=https://study.example.com
#
# Optional, with sensible defaults:
# SNOWPRO_LEGAL_JURISDICTION=India
# SNOWPRO_LEGAL_OPERATOR_FORM=an individual, not a registered company
# SNOWPRO_LEGAL_PRODUCT_NAME=SnowPro Core Prep
